],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"79fsd771","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"After the dough has risen, move the dough back onto the floured board, knead 4-5 times then cut it into 15 even pieces. I use a scale for this but I'm sure my grandma just eyeballed it and hers turned out just fine.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"ef0ln773","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":" ","decorations":[]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"utity775","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Form each of the 15 pieces into a ball and place them side by side evenly spaced in a 15\" x 9\" pan as shown above. They should be almost touching.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"vtnbv777","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":" ","decorations":[]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"kqzv6779","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Cover them loosely with plastic wrap sprayed with oil and allow the dough to rise once more in a warm place until doubled (45-75 minutes)","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"7ag3a781","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":" ","decorations":[]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"eg1ka783","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Start preheating an oven to 375 degrees.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"u4vyj785","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":" ","decorations":[]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"7q9n5787","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"When the dough has risen, gently brush the tops of the rolls with melted butter and place them in the oven.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"g7r8h789","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":" ","decorations":[]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"ibrai791","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Bake 30 - 40 minutes until the tops are golden. Brush again with melted butter and serve.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"transparent","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"}],"metadata":{"version":1.0,"createdTimestamp":"2023-02-17T23:06:49.876Z","updatedTimestamp":"2023-02-17T23:06:49.876Z","id":"2d526f56-3f28-47f8-b646-bb13f2516513"},"documentStyle":{}},"instructions":"\nAdd all of the dry ingredients and the butter to a stand mixer and mix on low speed just to combine, then add the sourdough starter and begin mixing on low speed. \n\n \n\nWith the mixer running on low speed slowly add the warm milk, water or whey, up to one cup until the dough begins to pull away from the sides of the bowl as shown above. If the dough has not fully combined, add warm liquid one tablespoon at a time until it does. Be sure to give it time to mix in before each addition. If the dough becomes too wet add a little more all purpose flour until it comes together.\n\n \n\nOnce your dough has come together, continue to mix for 6-8 more minutes on low. The dough should be fairly sticky.\n\n \n\nScrape the dough onto a well floured board and knead with floured hands 4-5 times and form the dough into a ball.\n\n \n\nPlace the ball into a bowl with 2 teaspoons of olive oil and swirl it around until all sides are lightly coated with the oil.\n\n \n\nCover with plastic wrap and leave the dough to rise in a warm place until doubled. (60-90 minutes)\n\n \n\nAfter the dough has risen, move the dough back onto the floured board, knead 4-5 times then cut it into 15 even pieces. I use a scale for this but I'm sure my grandma just eyeballed it and hers turned out just fine.\n\n \n\nForm each of the 15 pieces into a ball and place them side by side evenly spaced in a 15\" x 9\" pan as shown above. They should be almost touching.\n\n \n\nCover them loosely with plastic wrap sprayed with oil and allow the dough to rise once more in a warm place until doubled (45-75 minutes)\n\n \n\nStart preheating an oven to 375 degrees.\n\n \n\nWhen the dough has risen, gently brush the tops of the rolls with melted butter and place them in the oven.\n\n \n\nBake 30 - 40 minutes until the tops are golden. Brush again with melted butter and serve.","title":"Sourdough Pull-Apart Rolls"}}],"pagingMetadata":{"count":1,"offset":0,"tooManyToCount":false,"cursors":{"next":""},"hasNext":true}}